Project: Weather-Driven Avatar Dashboard (MVP)

Overview

* Short summary: A mobile/web/desktop dashboard that pulls local weather and area data to create actionable, location-relevant activities for users. Completing activities grants XP and items which modify a full-body avatar and its background — "a rendition of \[local] outside world" — that reflects the user's real-world environment and progress.

Goals (MVP)

* Fetch local weather + basic area data (temperature, precipitation, wind, air quality, sunrise/sunset).
* Show a dashboard with current conditions, 24-hour forecast, and suggested activities.
* Let users log activity completion to gain XP and receive items.
* Generate a full-body avatar and a background image representing the local outside world; allow basic dressing with items.
* Track inventory, XP, level, and item upgrades.

Key Features

* Data ingestion: Use device geolocation to request weather and area data from a local-weather API (e.g., Open-Meteo, OpenWeatherMap) or local sensors when available.
* Dashboard: Cards for current weather, short forecast, activity suggestions, nearby POI highlights, and avatar preview.
* Activities engine: Map weather/area data to prioritized activities (e.g., "Take a walk" when clear, "Wear a jacket" when cold). Each activity has XP reward and possible item drops.
* Progression: XP, levels, and item rarity tiers. Items combine into "even cooler items" through upgrade recipes.
* Avatar \& background: Full-body avatar renderer (2D or simple 3D) plus background composition that incorporates local landmarks or environmental cues from area data.

Data Model (high level)

* User: id, displayName, location (lat/lon), level, xp, inventory, equippedItems.
* WeatherSnapshot: timestamp, location, tempC, conditionCode, precipitationMm, windKph, aqi, sunrise, sunset.
* Activity: id, title, description, conditions (rules), xpReward, itemDrops.
* Item: id, name, tier, attributes (cosmetic tags), upgradeRecipe.

UX Flow (MVP)

1. On first-run request location permission.
2. Dashboard shows local weather + avatar preview.
3. App suggests 3-5 activities ranked by relevance.
4. User taps an activity to view details and logs completion when done.
5. On completion, grant XP and possibly items; update avatar/inventory.

Technical Notes

* Backend: Optional. MVP can be a client-first app using local storage and direct API calls to weather services. For multi-device sync, add a simple REST backend with user auth.
* APIs: Use geolocation APIs, Open-Meteo/OpenWeatherMap for weather, optional Places/POI APIs for landmarks.
* Avatar generation: Start with configurable 2D SVG avatars; upgrade to stylized rendered images later. Background can be procedurally composed from weather icons, time-of-day tints, and local landmark images when available.
* Privacy \& permissions: Only use coarse location unless user opts in to precise. Clearly document data retention and provide opt-out.

MVP Success Criteria

* App pulls correct local weather for user's location.
* Dashboard shows suggested activities that change with weather.
* Users can log activities and receive XP/items; avatar updates reflect inventory.
* Basic avatar customization works (equip/unequip items).

Next Steps

